# Digest 10: pattern 8, Delight or pride in what the child now does

Share: baby-development/_work/2026-11/digests-age-reread/share-10.md. Yes posts: 81. Near posts: 17.

## Pattern 8, share 10: yes posts

### The parent celebrates speech progress after having worried about how little their child could say.

7 of 81 posts.

At two to three years, parents celebrate longer sentences, clear conversations, recalling a day, a holiday greeting or a first spoken expression of love. Several explicitly describe speech delay or earlier worries and other people’s concerns; one had nine months of therapy, another is about to start, and another reports a large change during preschool. A smaller step can matter as much to these parents as fluent conversation.

- "When I woke up and came downstairs, my son runs up to me, hands me a card that he made, and says “happy mommy day!”" [ar033-0048]
- "He just turned 2.5 and 3 people in the last 24hours have asked how old he was and were in shock he was only 2.5 and commented how well and clearly he spoke for his age because he can carry out full convos now and is super clear when he speaks. Makes my mom heart so happy to hear people amazed by his verbal skills rather than concerned about it like they were a year ago 🥹" [ar055-0010]
- "He started saying “mama tété” (“tété” is the French word we use for nursing) — his first 2-word combination and he says it each time he wants to nurse!" [ar036-0004]
- "Sometimes progress isn’t a big milestone. Sometimes it’s hearing your little one excitedly tell you about their day before they drift off to sleep." [ar071-0061]
- "My daughter is 2 years old and normally just blows me a kiss to show her love, as she's always been a little behind on her speech but today she said "I love you mommy" ❤️❤️❤️ I sobbed happy tears" [ar067-0084]
- "My 2 year is speaking more 3 word sentences and get so happy when she does. She has recently started to say “I did it!” and answer “What is it?” or “I am here” when I call her name. 😭" [ar006-0010]
- "This boy NEVER stops talking. He has a massive vocabulary." [ar073-0061]

**But:** Celebration does not mean the delay has resolved. "He’s still quite behind with expressive speech, but we’ll finally start speech therapy in 2 weeks, and I’m really looking forward to it." [ar036-0006]

