At two to three years, children ask about bodies and pregnancy, apply familiar names to unfamiliar things, and explain discomfort or death in unexpected ways. Parents report literal descriptions, mistaken meanings and one alarming mishearing that turns out to be a wish to count people. The humour includes a child describing a bald head as an oval and another comparing a fart with a football commentator’s goal call.

