4 of 81 posts.

Parents of two- and three-year-olds hear an older sibling’s “bro,” self-directed praise, a book illustrator’s name used for Dad, and a parent’s habit of apologising to objects. They share the child’s comic timing and the way phrases acquire new uses. One asks how much children learn by copying adults who do not realise they are being watched.

