### The parent sees their child beginning to understand and manage feelings through their conversations and example.

3 of 81 posts.

An almost-three-year-old calms during reassurance and asks for a cuddle; a three-year-old copies the parent’s brief breaks and returns ready to talk. Another three-year-old answers an apology by saying they are still learning to be themselves. Parents connect these moments with how they comfort, model emotions or repair an overreaction.

- "So I stroked his head and I told him we love him, that I get it sucks to not be able to read unlimited books, that it's okay to cry it out, and that tomorrow and tomorrow and tomorrow we'll get to read more and spend more time together. And as I talked, he actually calmed down and came to." [ar069-0046]
- "It never lasts long, I'm in there 2 maybe 3 minutes max. Now when my son is upset he walks away from me and closes the door to "take a break", he opens the door when he's ready to talk about it." [ar038-0046]
- "She looked at me and said, “And I’m still learning how to be me.”" [ar066-0140]
- "And that maybe someday, he'll be able to comfort himself when he really needs it, remembering how his dad and I used to when he was a kid." [ar069-0048]

**But:** The parent does not report that comfort works every time: "Not every time I try to comfort my kid do I actually get through to him. That would be impossible." [ar069-0048]

